Reactive Arthritis
Rheumatology & Immunology
Preceding infection, Asymmetric lower limb oligoarthritis, Conjunctivitis/Uveitis

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-038
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of a massively swollen, painful knee and ankle, combined with a severely red, painful eye and pain on urination, rendering the patient completely unable to walk.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with acute, severe lower limb oligoarthritis causing sudden immobility, complicated by acute ocular and genitourinary symptoms. Urgent after-hours bedside assessment was mandated to rule out an acute gonococcal or non-gonococcal septic arthritis, assess the severity of the ocular inflammation (potential sight-threatening uveitis), and provide immediate immobilizing analgesia, as waiting until standard hours carried high risks of joint destruction, permanent visual impairment, and unmanageable distress.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My right knee is blown up like a balloon, I can't put any weight on my left heel, my right eye is burning and bright red, and it burns when I pee."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a rapid, 48-hour history of acute, asymmetric oligoarthritis, typically affecting the weight-bearing joints (massive right knee effusion and severe left ankle pain). Describes exquisite pain at the back of the left heel (Achilles tendinitis indicating severe enthesitis), completely preventing weight-bearing. Upon direct questioning, the patient admits to a preceding bout of severe gastroenteritis (Campylobacter) approximately 3 weeks prior. Today, the patient developed conjunctivitis presenting with a red, painful right eye. Furthermore, complains of new-onset sterile urethritis causing severe dysuria, and notes painless, superficial ulcers on the glans penis (circinate balanitis). Denies any current diarrhea or high swinging fevers.
Functional Status: Strictly bedbound; attempting to walk causes agonizing pain in the knee and heel.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; unable to access food or mobilize safely due to the combination of severe joint and heel pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 mmHg | HR: 100 bpm | Temp: 37.7 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Patient appears highly distressed, lying supine, guarding the right leg and shielding the right eye from ambient light.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal: The right knee has a massive, tense, warm effusion. Range of motion is severely restricted (10-80 degrees) due to capsular distension. Exquisite point tenderness is present at the insertion of the left Achilles tendon. The left 2nd toe is uniformly swollen and erythematous (dactylitis). HEENT: The right eye exhibits profound bulbar conjunctival injection, watery discharge, and mild photophobia. Genitourinary: (Patient self-reports or visual inspection confirms) characteristic shallow, painless, serpiginous ulcers on the glans penis (circinate balanitis). Dermatological: Soles of the feet show early hyperkeratotic, painless skin lesions (keratoderma blennorrhagicum). The diagnostic tetrad is completely, classically fulfilled: clear preceding infection (gastroenteritis), profound asymmetric lower limb oligoarthritis, and prominent conjunctivitis, anticipating sterile inflammatory synovial fluid on future joint aspiration and potential positive HLA-B27 genetic testing.
Environmental Observations: Blinds drawn due to eye discomfort; a bedside urinal is in use.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking paracetamol with absolutely no relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Reactive Arthritis - Status: Exacerbated (Acute multi-system flare)
Differentials:

Disseminated Gonococcal Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of the classic triad (arthritis, urethritis, conjunctivitis) following a known gastrointestinal trigger, and the specific presence of keratoderma and circinate balanitis, which are pathognomonic for Reactive Arthritis rather than gonococcemia.

Septic Arthritis (Non-gonococcal) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinctly polyarticular/asymmetric pattern (knee, ankle, heel, toe), the prominent systemic mucocutaneous and ocular involvement, and the absence of rigors or a high swinging fever.

Acute Gout - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of urethritis, conjunctivitis, and specific skin lesions, which are entirely absent in crystal arthropathy.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Acute mobility failure)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an archetypal, severe case of Reactive Arthritis (formerly Reiter's syndrome), triggered by a recent gastrointestinal infection. The profound acute monoarthritis of the knee combined with severe enthesitis of the heel has caused absolute functional immobilization. Urgent after-hours assessment was critical to physically examine the joint to safely exclude an isolated septic arthritis, assess the eye for sight-threatening anterior uveitis, and immediately instigate high-dose systemic NSAIDs. Leaving the patient unmedicated and unassessed overnight posed unacceptable risks of ocular damage and severe, unmanageable pain, firmly validating the MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiate an immediate, scheduled, high-dose NSAID regimen (e.g., Indomethacin 50mg TID or Naproxen) with a proton pump inhibitor to aggressively suppress the severe systemic inflammation. The patient requires an urgent referral to an ophthalmologist (within 24 hours) to rule out acute anterior uveitis, and an urgent rheumatology referral. Recommend formal joint aspiration (arthrocentesis) via the GP or ED to definitively confirm sterile fluid if the effusion does not rapidly improve.
Positive Behavioral Change: Strict joint offloading: Utilize crutches to completely avoid bearing weight on the left heel and right knee; demonstrating that avoiding the mechanical compression of the inflamed Achilles tendon immediately prevents the sharp, agonizing pain, heavily reinforcing the need for strict rest. Ocular protection: Wear dark sunglasses indoors constantly; linking this simple physical barrier to immediate relief from the photophobic eye pain.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None immediately.
DME/Safety (Optional): Advised to arrange delivery of crutches or a walking frame tomorrow.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 24 hours for joint aspiration consideration and STI screening (to rule out concurrent chlamydia). Urgent Ophthalmology review required.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the right knee becomes intensely hot and bright red, if you develop a high swinging fever with uncontrollable shivering, or if the vision in your right eye suddenly becomes very blurry or completely dark.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The severe combination of your swollen joints, inflamed eye, and urinary pain is a massive, reactive immune response to the stomach bug you had weeks ago. While not a direct joint infection, the inflammation is severe and requires strong medication.
Clinical Course: With high-dose anti-inflammatories, the acute symptoms usually begin to improve within a week, but the condition can take several months to completely resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to walk without support. If you start shivering violently, develop a high fever, or if your eye pain becomes agonizing with a sudden loss of vision, you must go to the emergency room immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system successfully fought off the stomach bacteria weeks ago, but it has now become confused and is aggressively attacking your own joints, eyes, and urinary tract. The strong anti-inflammatories will chemically force this misguided immune attack to stop. The fluid in your knee is sterile, not infected.
Actionable Steps: Take the Indomethacin strictly with food every 8 hours. Wear sunglasses indoors to rest the eye. See your GP tomorrow for urgent referrals and blood tests.

